WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Part 4. You will hear a lecturer on a languages course talking about the impact of digital technology on Icelandic, the native language of Iceland.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:45.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Right everyone, let's make a start.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Over the past few sessions, we've been considering the reasons why some world languages are in decline,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
and today I'm going to introduce another factor that affects languages and the speakers of those languages,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
and that's technology, and in particular, digital technology.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.000
In order to illustrate its effect, I'm going to focus on the Icelandic language,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:51.000
which is spoken by around 321,000 people, most of whom live in Iceland, an island in the North Atlantic Ocean.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
The problem for this language is not the number of speakers, even though this number is small,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
nor is it about losing words to other languages, such as English.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
In fact, the vocabulary of Icelandic is continually increasing,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.000
because when speakers need a new word for something, they tend to create one, rather than borrowing from another language.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.000
All this makes Icelandic quite a special language.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:26.000
It's changed very little in the past millennium, yet it can handle 21st century concepts related to the use of computers and digital technology.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Take, for example, the word for web browser.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
This is vafri in Icelandic, which comes from the verb to wander.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000
I can't think of a more appropriate term, because that's exactly what you do mentally when you browse the internet.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Then there's an Icelandic word for podcast, which is too hard to pronounce, and so on.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Icelandic, then, is alive and growing, but, and it's a big but,

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
young Icelanders spend a great deal of time in the digital world, and this world is predominantly English.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Think about smartphones.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.000
They didn't even exist until comparatively recently,

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:15.000
but today, young people use them all the time to read books, watch TV or films, play games, listen to music, and so on.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Obviously, this is a good thing in many respects, because it promotes their bilingual skills,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
but the extent of the influence of English in the virtual world is staggering, and it's all happening really fast.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
For their parents and grandparents, the change is less concerning,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.000
because they already have their native speaker skills in Icelandic,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.000
but for young speakers, well, the outcome is a little troubling.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:49.000
For example, teachers have found that playground conversations in Icelandic secondary schools can be conducted entirely in English,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.000
while teachers of much younger children have reported situations

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.000
where their classes find it easier to say what is in a picture using English rather than Icelandic.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:08.000
The very real and worrying consequence of all this is that the young generation in Iceland is at risk of losing its mother tongue.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Of course, this is happening to other European languages too,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:22.000
but while internet companies might be willing to offer, say, French options in their systems,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:31.000
it's much harder for them to justify the expense of doing the same for a language that has a population the size of a French town, such as Nice.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:38.000
The other drawback of Icelandic is the grammar, which is significantly more complex than in most languages.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.000
At the moment, the tech giants are simply not interested in tackling this.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.000
So, what is the Icelandic government doing about this?

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Well, large sums of money are being allocated to a language technology fund

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.000
that it is hoped will lead to the development of Icelandic-sourced apps and other social media and digital systems,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.000
but clearly this is going to be an uphill struggle.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.000
On the positive side, they know that Icelandic is still the official language of education and government.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.000
It has survived for well over a thousand years,

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:19.000
and the experts predict that its future in this nation-state is sound and will continue to be so.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:25.000
However, there's no doubt that it's becoming an inevitable second choice in young people's lives.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.000
This raises important questions.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.000
When you consider how much of the past is tied up in a language,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.000
will young Icelanders lose their sense of their own identity?

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Another issue that concerns the government of Iceland is this.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.000
If children are learning two languages through different routes,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:50.000
neither of which they are fully fluent in, will they be able to express themselves properly?

